<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/"><channel><title>Generics on Beyond Swift</title><link>https://beyondswift.com/tags/generics/</link><description>Recent content in Generics on Beyond Swift</description><generator>Hugo -- 0.163.3</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Mon, 22 Sep 2025 11:07:00 +0530</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://beyondswift.com/tags/generics/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>any vs some: what a protocol type costs</title><link>https://beyondswift.com/protocols-shared-behavior-without-inheritance/</link><pubDate>Mon, 22 Sep 2025 11:07:00 +0530</pubDate><guid>https://beyondswift.com/protocols-shared-behavior-without-inheritance/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Two functions do the same job over a list of activities. One takes &lt;code&gt;[any LoggableActivity]&lt;/code&gt;, the other is generic over &lt;code&gt;[T]&lt;/code&gt;. They compile to very different machine code. Which one allocates a box per element, and which one gets the calorie math inlined?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;protocol&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;LoggableActivity&lt;/span&gt; {
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; caloriesBurned: Double { &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt; }
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; items: [any LoggableActivity]) -&amp;gt; Double { &lt;span style="color:#75715e"&gt;// A&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; items.reduce(&lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;) { $0 &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; $1.caloriesBurned }
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&amp;lt;T: LoggableActivity&amp;gt;(&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; items: [T]) -&amp;gt; Double { &lt;span style="color:#75715e"&gt;// B&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; items.reduce(&lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;) { $0 &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; $1.caloriesBurned }
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Version A pays for a heap box and a dynamic call on every element. Version B specializes to the concrete type and often inlines the whole loop. By the end you will know what &lt;code&gt;any&lt;/code&gt; actually stores, why &lt;code&gt;some&lt;/code&gt; and generics are cheaper, and when the box is the right call anyway.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>